    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The electoral college, a relic of the 18th century, has long been a contentious institution in the American electoral process, perpetuating a system that fundamentally diverges from the principles of modern democracy. By vesting disproportionate influence in smaller states and awarding electoral votes based on a winner-takes-all approach, the electoral college effectively disenfranchises millions of voters in densely populated urban centers, where the majority of the country's population resides. This antiquated system not only undermines the democratic ideal of one person, one vote, but also creates a situation in which the outcome of presidential elections is increasingly determined by the preferences of a handful of swing states, rendering the votes of citizens in the rest of the country - including those in states like California, New York, and Illinois - effectively irrelevant. As a result, the electoral college system has become a de facto tool of plutocratic governance, where the interests of rural voters are prioritized over those of urban, suburban, and minority populations, effectively disenfranchising the majority of Americans.",
      "source": "target",
